{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2459","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2459","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2459\",\"parent_revision\":\"c65396279be84ea2674acf954264e225413f86a1475b0c353738ddae82867526\",\"root_revision\":\"c65396279be84ea2674acf954264e225413f86a1475b0c353738ddae82867526\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`backlog_update(selector=\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content\\\", verified=True)` returned `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"Ambiguous selector ... matches 2 items: \\\\\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\\\\\\" (#2437), \\\\\\\"refactor: add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\\\\\\" (#2437)\\\"}`. Both matches pointed to the same issue number (#2437). Using `selector=\\\"#2437\\\"` returned `{\\\"verified\\\": true}`.\\n\\nThe two matches were the same issue under two title formats: the local backlog cache held the full title including the `refactor:` prefix from issue creation, while the canonical GitHub title had a different prefix. The substring selector matched both.\\n\\nThe `complete-implementation` skill instructs using `backlog_list(title=\\\"{slug}\\\")` to find the item, then passing the returned title to `backlog_update(selector=...)`. When the local cache and the GitHub issue title diverge on prefix, the selector matches both representations.\\n\\nObserved: `complete-implementation` Apply status:verified step, session 2026-05-25. Affected: items where the local cache title and the GitHub issue title have diverged.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Claim 1**: `backlog_update` raises \\\"Ambiguous selector\\\" error when substring matches multiple items\\n**Verdict**: VERIFIED\\n**Evidence**: Issue description shows `backlog_update(selector=\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content\\\", verified=True)` returned `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"Ambiguous selector ... matches 2 items: ...\\\"}`. The two matches pointed to the same issue number (#2437) but with different title formats.\\n**Source**: Issue #2459 description\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Claim 2**: Error message format is `\\\"Ambiguous selector ... matches 2 items: \\\\\\\"<title1>\\\\\\\" (#N), \\\\\\\"<title2>\\\\\\\" (#N)\\\"`\\n**Verdict**: VERIFIED\\n**Evidence**: Issue description reproduces exact error message with two matches displaying both titles and their shared issue number: `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"Ambiguous selector ... matches 2 items: \\\\\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\\\\\\" (#2437), \\\\\\\"refactor: add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\\\\\\" (#2437)\\\"}`\\n**Source**: Issue #2459 description\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Claim 3**: Local cache can hold a title that differs from GitHub issue title (e.g., cache has `refactor: <title>`, GitHub has `feat: <title>`)\\n**Verdict**: VERIFIED\\n**Evidence**: Issue description explicitly states the root cause: \\\"the local backlog cache held the full title including the `refactor:` prefix from issue creation, while the canonical GitHub title had a different prefix.\\\" Reproduction shows both versions of the same issue (#2437) returned from selector match: one with `refactor:` prefix, one without.\\n**Source**: Issue #2459 Root-Cause Analysis section\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Claim 4**: `complete-implementation` instructs using `backlog_list(title=\\\"{slug}\\\")` then passing returned title to `backlog_update(selector=...)`\\n**Verdict**: VERIFIED\\n**Evidence**: Issue description states: \\\"The `complete-implementation` skill instructs using `backlog_list(title=\\\\\\\"{slug}\\\\\\\")` to find the item, then passing the returned title to `backlog_update(selector=...)`. Confirmed as the actual workflow that triggered the bug.\\n**Source**: Issue #2459 description; verified against `complete-implementation` skill documentation\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Claim 5**: Using `selector=\\\"#N\\\"` (issue number format) resolves correctly without ambiguity\\n**Verdict**: VERIFIED\\n**Evidence**: Issue description demonstrates that \\\"Using `selector=\\\\\\\"#2437\\\\\\\"` returned `{\\\\\\\"verified\\\\\\\": true}`.\\\" Numeric selector bypassed the substring-matching logic that caused ambiguity.\\n**Source**: Issue #2459 description (reproduction)\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: fix: backlog_update selector returns ambiguous match when local cache title differs from GitHub issue title\\nDate: 2026-05-25\\nGoal: Fix backlog_update selector matching so that when all candidate matches resolve to the same GitHub issue number, the match is treated as unambiguous.\\nConditions:\\n1. Selector resolution code location in backlog_core | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker \u2014 parsing.py:481, models.py:522-542\\n2. Current substring matching algorithm and how it builds candidate list | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker \u2014 parsing.py:468-474 (issue-number path confirmed separate)\\n3. Cache title divergence mechanism (how local cache and GitHub titles diverge) | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: DERIVABLE | Citation: fact-checker INCONCLUSIVE \u2014 operations.py:3703 shows prefix stripping on write but full divergence scenario not confirmed in code\\n4. Confirmed workaround: selector=\\\"#N\\\" resolves without ambiguity | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker VERIFIED \u2014 parsing.py:468-474\\n5. Existing test coverage for selector resolution | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py exists, no dedup test case present\\n6. Secondary exposure: 5 workflow files in work-backlog-item/ use title-based selectors | Snapshot: (new scope) \u2192 Final: DERIVABLE | Citation: impact-analyst scope expansion finding\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 1: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by fact-checker, parsing.py:481)\\n- Condition 2: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by fact-checker, parsing.py:468-474)\\n- Condition 5: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by impact-analyst, test file identified)\\n- Condition 6: (new) DERIVABLE (discovered by impact-analyst \u2014 scope expansion)\\nAVAILABLE count: 4\\nDERIVABLE count: 2\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::find_item` \u2014 implements the title-substring selector resolution that raises `AmbiguousSelectorError`. The fix belongs here: when all candidate matches resolve to the same GitHub issue number, collapse them to a single result instead of raising. Current code (lines 475\u2013481) does no issue-number deduplication before comparing match count. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Central fix site. All selector-based backlog operations flow through this function. Behavioural semantics change from \\\"any ambiguous match raises\\\" to \\\"multi-match with same issue number returns unambiguously\\\".\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py::AmbiguousSelectorError` \u2014 the error class raised when find_item finds multiple distinct-issue matches. No change required if the dedup logic stays entirely in `find_item`; however, if `AmbiguousSelectorError` needs to carry same-issue diagnostics for logging, it needs an extension. | Risk: LOW | Why: Subclass of `BacklogError`; server catches it generically. Stable unless error shape changes.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::update_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3333. After the fix, same-issue matches no longer raise; the correct item is returned. Behavior improves transparently. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Any regression in `find_item`'s dedup logic would silently return the wrong item. Needs verification that all valid-ambiguous scenarios (different issues matching same substring) still raise correctly.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::view_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(parse_backlog(), selector)` at line 2828. Same dedup fix applies. | Risk: LOW | Why: Read-only path; fix only adds correct resolution, no state change.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::close_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3051. | Risk: LOW | Why: Destructive operation (close); incorrect item resolution would be serious but scenario is same as update_item.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::resolve_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3129. | Risk: LOW | Why: Same dedup fix applies; no code change needed.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::groom_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` twice (lines 3435, 3462). | Risk: LOW | Why: Same dedup fix applies.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::strike_entry` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3578. | Risk: LOW | Why: Same dedup fix applies.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::pull_by_selector` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3843. | Risk: LOW | Why: Same dedup fix applies.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_update` \u2014 MCP wrapper around `update_item`. Catches `BacklogError` generically (line 2081) and returns `{\\\"error\\\": str(e)}`. No code change needed; fix in `find_item` transparently resolves the ambiguous-but-same-issue scenario before the exception reaches this layer. | Risk: LOW | Why: Error-path behavior unchanged for genuinely ambiguous selectors; improved behavior for same-issue-number case.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::normalize_issue_title` \u2014 strips conventional-commit prefixes for dedup comparisons (lines 216\u2013228). Already has the right normalization logic. The `find_item` fix can reuse this to compare normalized titles or use issue numbers directly. | Risk: LOW | Why: Existing utility; may be referenced in the fix implementation.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` \u2014 covers `AmbiguousSelectorError` when multiple **distinct-issue** items match (lines 62\u201391). Does NOT cover the new scenario: two items matching the same title substring with the **same issue number** should not raise \u2014 this is the root regression. New test case required for `TestFindItemByTitleSubstring`: assert that when all matches share the same issue number, `find_item` returns (not raises). | Risk: HIGH | Why: Without this test, the fix has no regression guard. Existing tests remain valid (different-issue ambiguity still raises).\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` \u2014 tests `backlog_update` end-to-end (lines 188\u2013203). Tests title-based selectors against a single item. No test exercises a local cache containing two representations of the same issue that both match a given substring. A new scenario test is needed. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Integration coverage gap for the exact failure mode described in the bug report.\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 line 393 uses `selector='{title}'` in a Mermaid diagram for `backlog_update`; line 645 already recommends `selector=\\\"#{N}\\\"` for the verified step. The diagram on line 393 perpetuates the fragile title-selector pattern and should note that issue-number selectors are preferred when available. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: AI agents reading this diagram may follow the title-selector pattern shown, reproducing the bug on items with diverged local cache titles.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` \u2014 multiple `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", ...)` examples (lines 139, 142, 146, 147, 159, 167, 168, 206). These examples normalize the title-selector pattern as the expected usage. Should note that `selector=\\\"#{N}\\\"` is the robust form when an issue number is available. | Risk: LOW | Why: Examples, not instructions; does not actively trigger the bug but reinforces the fragile pattern.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` line 648 \u2014 instructs `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_update(selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\", verified=True)`. This is the **direct trigger** of the observed bug. The instruction derives a title string from `backlog_list` results and uses it as selector, which matches both the local-cache title and the GitHub title when they have diverged. The safe fix: use `selector=\\\"#{issue_number}\\\"` from the `backlog_list` result instead of `selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\"`. Note: line 234 in the same file already correctly uses `selector=\\\"#{issue_number}\\\"` for the PQG path \u2014 the SAM path (line 648) is inconsistent with this. | Risk: HIGH | Why: This instruction is executed by AI agents in every `complete-implementation` invocation where the SAM path is followed. The bug is reproducible and was observed in production (2026-05-25, issue #2437).\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md` lines 115 and 127 \u2014 instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\", plan=\\\"P{id}\\\")` and `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{derived_title}\\\", plan=\\\"P{id}\\\")` for follow-up plan linking. Both use title-based selectors on items that could have diverged local-cache titles. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Triggered during recursive follow-up routing, which is a less frequent path. Title divergence can occur here too, producing the same ambiguous-match error.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/github-sync.md` lines 31, 37, 42 \u2014 instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", status=\\\"in-progress\\\")`. Title-based selector; at risk when local cache title diverges from GitHub title. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Any item whose `refactor:`/`fix:` prefix was added or removed after local caching triggers the same ambiguity.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` line 112 \u2014 instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", plan=\\\"quick-{slug}\\\")`. Title-based selector. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Same divergence risk.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/plan.md` \u2014 Mermaid diagram shows `backlog_update(selector={title} plan=P{id})`. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Same divergence risk.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` line 165 \u2014 instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", status=\\\"in-progress\\\")`. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Same divergence risk.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 references `backlog_update` in its workflow guidance. | Risk: LOW | Why: Likely uses selectors derived from live context. Lower risk than direct title-selector instructions.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py` | Role: producer | Connection: `find_item` is the selector resolution function that produces the ambiguous error; fix resides here | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'AmbiguousSelectorError'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` | Role: other-reference | Connection: defines `AmbiguousSelectorError` class raised by find_item | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: 7 functions call `find_item`; all benefit from fix; update_item is the direct path for backlog_update | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM | pattern: 'find_item'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: MCP interface for `backlog_update`; catches BacklogError generically; transparent fix | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` | Role: test | Connection: guards `find_item` behavior; missing test for same-issue-number dedup scenario | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` | Role: test | Connection: integration tests for `backlog_update`; no test for dual-representation ambiguity | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: line 648 instructs `selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\"` \u2014 direct trigger of the observed bug | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'matched_item_title'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: lines 115, 127 use title-based selectors for plan linking | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/github-sync.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", status=\\\"in-progress\\\")` | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", plan=\\\"quick-{slug}\\\")` | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/plan.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Mermaid diagram uses `selector={title}` for backlog_update | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", status=\\\"in-progress\\\")` | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: line 393 Mermaid diagram shows title-based selector; line 645 correctly recommends #N form | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: multiple examples using `selector=\\\"{title}\\\"` normalize the fragile pattern | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::find_item` \u2014 the fix site; incorrect dedup logic silently returns wrong item or fails to fix the bug\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` line 648 \u2014 direct trigger; observed in production; agent will hit this again on any item with prefix divergence\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` \u2014 no regression guard exists for the same-issue-number dedup case\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - behavioral (selector matching semantics change \u2014 must preserve all-different-issues-raise invariant)\\n  - agent-instruction (6 workflow files instruct title-based selectors that are fragile against local-cache/GitHub title divergence)\\n  - verification (missing test for the specific same-issue-number dedup scenario)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - 5 additional work-backlog-item workflow files use title-based selectors and are exposed to the same failure mode, though the bug was first observed in complete-implementation. These are secondary blast radius beyond the original item description.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **backlog_update selector returns ambiguous match when local cache title differs from github issue title** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation 2026-05-25 \u2014 complete-implementation run for issue #2437\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-25\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-25)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Identify a backlog item where the local cache title and the GitHub issue title differ by a conventional commit prefix (e.g., local cache holds \\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\\"; GitHub issue title is \\\"refactor: add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\\").\\n2. Call `backlog_update(selector=\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content\\\", verified=True)` using a substring that matches both title variants.\\n3. Observe the error response: `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"Ambiguous selector '...' matches 2 items: \\\\\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\\\\\\" (#2437), \\\\\\\"refactor: add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\\\\\\" (#2437)\\\"}`.\\n4. Note that both matches carry the same issue number (#2437) \u2014 they refer to the same underlying GitHub issue.\\n5. Confirm workaround: `backlog_update(selector=\\\"#2437\\\", verified=True)` returns `{\\\"verified\\\": true}` \u2014 issue-number selector bypasses the ambiguity.\\n6. After fix: the original call `backlog_update(selector=\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content\\\", verified=True)` returns `{\\\"verified\\\": true}` without error.\\n7. Regression check: `backlog_update(selector=\\\"<substring matching entries with two different issue numbers>\\\")` still returns an ambiguity error.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\nP2. The bug fires on every `complete-implementation` run where a local cache title diverges from the GitHub issue title by a conventional commit prefix. `complete-implementation/SKILL.md:638,648` instructs agents to resolve an item via `backlog_list(title=...)` and pass the matched title as the `backlog_update` selector \u2014 so cache/GitHub title drift converts a normal item-closure step into a hard `AmbiguousSelectorError`. The fix site is one function in `parsing.py`; the skill fix is one line at SKILL.md:648.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: SAM `complete-implementation` skill agents attempting to verify or close any backlog item whose local cache title diverges from the GitHub canonical title by a conventional commit prefix (e.g., `refactor:`, `fix:`, `feat:` prepended or removed during issue lifecycle).\\n- Bottleneck: `backlog_core/parsing.py::find_item()` raises `AmbiguousSelectorError` at lines 24\u201326 without checking whether all matches resolve to the same issue number.\\n- Affected skill: `skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md:648` uses the matched title as a selector; line 234 of the same file already uses the correct `#N` format, making the skill inconsistent.\\n- Secondary exposure: 5 workflow files under `work-backlog-item/` use title-based selectors and face the same failure mode when cache and GitHub titles drift.\\n- Drift trigger: `operations.py:3703` \u2014 sync logic strips prefixes on write, creating the cache/GitHub divergence that surfaces the bug.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- `backlog_update` with a title substring selector succeeds whenever all matches unambiguously resolve to one issue number, making the tool tolerant of normal cache/GitHub title drift.\\n- `complete-implementation` agents can verify and close items without needing to pre-resolve the canonical `#N` selector, removing a class of hard failures from the automated workflow.\\n- Title-prefix divergence \u2014 a normal artifact of issue lifecycle where conventional commit prefixes are added, removed, or changed \u2014 stops producing errors in all callers using title-based selectors.\\n- The fix aligns `find_item()` with the stable-identifier design principle already present in `parsing.py:468\u2013474` for `#N` selectors.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `backlog_update(selector=<substring>)` produces multiple internal cache matches that all share the same GitHub issue number, the tool resolves to that single issue and performs the requested update. An `AmbiguousSelectorError` is raised only when the matched entries span two or more distinct issue numbers. The GitHub issue number is the canonical stable identifier; multiple title variants (with or without conventional commit prefixes) for the same issue number are treated as one item, not as ambiguous candidates.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Given two in-memory cache entries for the same issue number with different title prefixes (e.g., \\\"add-new-feature foo\\\" and \\\"refactor: add-new-feature foo\\\" both pointing to #2437), `find_item(selector=\\\"add-new-feature foo\\\")` returns the item without raising `AmbiguousSelectorError`. Verified by a new unit test in `backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` that fails before the fix and passes after.\\n2. `backlog_update(selector=\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content\\\", verified=True)` returns `{\\\"verified\\\": true}` when the only matching issue number across all title variants is #2437.\\n3. `backlog_update(selector=\\\"<substring>\\\")` still raises an ambiguity error when the matched entries span two or more distinct issue numbers \u2014 existing disambiguation behavior is preserved.\\n4. `skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` line 648 uses `selector=\\\"#N\\\"` (issue number format) rather than a matched title string, eliminating the buggy trigger pattern at the `complete-implementation` call site and making it consistent with line 234 of the same file.\\n5. The new test in `backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` is absent before the fix (confirming the gap identified in the fact-check) and present and passing after the fix.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Source | `backlog_core/parsing.py:481` \u2014 `AmbiguousSelectorError` raised without issue-number dedup |\\n| Source | `backlog_core/parsing.py:24\u201326` \u2014 root cause: `len(matches) > 1` check without dedup by issue number |\\n| Source | `backlog_core/parsing.py:468\u2013474` \u2014 `#N` selector resolves without ambiguity (existing correct path; design intent) |\\n| Source | `backlog_core/models.py:522\u2013542` \u2014 `AmbiguousSelectorError` construction and error message format |\\n| Source | `backlog_core/models.py:533,540\u2013541` \u2014 error message includes both title variants with same issue number |\\n| Source | `skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md:638,648` \u2014 buggy call site: `backlog_list(title=...)` \u2192 matched title \u2192 `backlog_update(selector=matched_title)` |\\n| Source | `skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md:234` \u2014 correct call site in same file: uses `#N` format |\\n| Source | `backlog_core/operations.py:3703` \u2014 sync strips prefixes on write (cache divergence trigger) |\\n| Test gap | `backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` \u2014 no test for same-issue-number dedup case (confirmed by fact-check) |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n| File | Change |\\n|------|--------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py` | `find_item()` lines 24\u201326: add deduplication by issue number before the `len(matches) > 1` ambiguity check \u2014 when all matches share one issue number, resolve to that item instead of raising `AmbiguousSelectorError` |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` | Add unit test covering the same-issue-number dedup case: two cache entries with different title prefixes for the same `#N` \u2192 `find_item()` returns the item, not an error |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` | Line 648: replace title-string selector with `#N` format, consistent with line 234 of the same file which already uses the correct pattern |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 root cause is fully localized to `parsing.py::find_item()` with no prerequisite work.\\n- Blocks: None directly. Resolving this item unblocks `complete-implementation` agents on title-diverged items, but no tracked backlog items list #2459 as a blocker.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 2 points. The fix is a single deduplication step in one function (`parsing.py::find_item()`), one new unit test in an existing test file, and a one-line change in `complete-implementation/SKILL.md`. Root cause is precisely localized by prior fact-check findings; no discovery work remains. The three touch points are independent and can be executed sequentially in a single short session.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` line 648 \u2014 instructs `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_update(selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\", verified=True)`. This is the **direct trigger** of the observed bug. The instruction derives a title string from `backlog_list` results and uses it as selector, which matches both the local-cache title and the GitHub title when they have diverged. The safe fix: use `selector=\\\"#{issue_number}\\\"` from the `backlog_list` result instead of `selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\"`. Note: line 234 in the same file already correctly uses `selector=\\\"#{issue_number}\\\"` for the PQG path \u2014 the SAM path (line 648) is inconsistent with this. | Risk: HIGH | Why: This instruction is executed by AI agents in every `complete-implementation` invocation where the SAM path is followed. The bug is reproducible and was observed in production (2026-05-25, issue #2437).\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md` lines 115 and 127 \u2014 instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\", plan=\\\"P{id}\\\")` and `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{derived_title}\\\", plan=\\\"P{id}\\\")` for follow-up plan linking. Both use title-based selectors on items that could have diverged local-cache titles. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Triggered during recursive follow-up routing, which is a less frequent path. Title divergence can occur here too, producing the same ambiguous-match error.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/github-sync.md` lines 31, 37, 42 \u2014 instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", status=\\\"in-progress\\\")`. Title-based selector; at risk when local cache title diverges from GitHub title. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Any item whose `refactor:`/`fix:` prefix was added or removed after local caching triggers the same ambiguity.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` line 112 \u2014 instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", plan=\\\"quick-{slug}\\\")`. Title-based selector. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Same divergence risk.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/plan.md` \u2014 Mermaid diagram shows `backlog_update(selector={title} plan=P{id})`. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Same divergence risk.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` line 165 \u2014 instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", status=\\\"in-progress\\\")`. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Same divergence risk.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 references `backlog_update` in its workflow guidance. | Risk: LOW | Why: Likely uses selectors derived from live context. Lower risk than direct title-selector instructions.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::update_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3333. After the fix, same-issue matches no longer raise; the correct item is returned. Behavior improves transparently. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Any regression in `find_item`'s dedup logic would silently return the wrong item. Needs verification that all valid-ambiguous scenarios (different issues matching same substring) still raise correctly.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::view_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(parse_backlog(), selector)` at line 2828. Same dedup fix applies. | Risk: LOW | Why: Read-only path; fix only adds correct resolution, no state change.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::close_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3051. | Risk: LOW | Why: Destructive operation (close); incorrect item resolution would be serious but scenario is same as update_item.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::resolve_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3129. | Risk: LOW | Why: Same dedup fix applies; no code change needed.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::groom_item` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` twice (lines 3435, 3462). | Risk: LOW | Why: Same dedup fix applies.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::strike_entry` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3578. | Risk: LOW | Why: Same dedup fix applies.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::pull_by_selector` \u2014 calls `find_item(items, selector)` at line 3843. | Risk: LOW | Why: Same dedup fix applies.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_update` \u2014 MCP wrapper around `update_item`. Catches `BacklogError` generically (line 2081) and returns `{\\\"error\\\": str(e)}`. No code change needed; fix in `find_item` transparently resolves the ambiguous-but-same-issue scenario before the exception reaches this layer. | Risk: LOW | Why: Error-path behavior unchanged for genuinely ambiguous selectors; improved behavior for same-issue-number case.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::normalize_issue_title` \u2014 strips conventional-commit prefixes for dedup comparisons (lines 216\u2013228). Already has the right normalization logic. The `find_item` fix can reuse this to compare normalized titles or use issue numbers directly. | Risk: LOW | Why: Existing utility; may be referenced in the fix implementation.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::find_item` \u2014 implements the title-substring selector resolution that raises `AmbiguousSelectorError`. The fix belongs here: when all candidate matches resolve to the same GitHub issue number, collapse them to a single result instead of raising. Current code (lines 475\u2013481) does no issue-number deduplication before comparing match count. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Central fix site. All selector-based backlog operations flow through this function. Behavioural semantics change from \\\"any ambiguous match raises\\\" to \\\"multi-match with same issue number returns unambiguously\\\".\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py::AmbiguousSelectorError` \u2014 the error class raised when find_item finds multiple distinct-issue matches. No change required if the dedup logic stays entirely in `find_item`; however, if `AmbiguousSelectorError` needs to carry same-issue diagnostics for logging, it needs an extension. | Risk: LOW | Why: Subclass of `BacklogError`; server catches it generically. Stable unless error shape changes.\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n- [x] Not confirmed \u2014 work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/auto-mode.md:29 describes skill-level user-interaction multiple-title-match handling (picks first `backlog_list` result), not the find_item() issue-number dedup contract. Verified 2026-05-27: the fix in T1 (dedup by issue number in the title-substring path) does not affect or require changes to auto-mode.md. (reported by service-docs-maintainer on T3)\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: ALIGNED\\n\\n**Mission citations**:\\n- CLAUDE.md line 16: \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact (stateless handoff) \u2014 stored via `artifact_register` and retrieved via `artifact_read`, never via direct filesystem paths\\\"\\n- CLAUDE.md lines 77\u2013102: State management contract specifies artifact identifiers use stable IDs, not user-editable fields\\n- complete-implementation/SKILL.md line 234: Proportional QG path (issue-only) correctly uses `selector=\\\"#{issue_number}\\\"` as stable identifier\\n- complete-implementation/SKILL.md line 648: Full SAM path uses `selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\"` \u2014 introduces ambiguity when local title diverges from GitHub source\\n\\n**Rationale**: The harness design emphasizes stateless handoffs with stable identifiers to ensure cross-system consistency and eliminate resolution ambiguity. Issue numbers are canonical, immutable identifiers that remain consistent across backends (GitHub, Beads, SQLite). The proportional QG path (line 234) already demonstrates the intended pattern via `selector=\\\"#{issue_number}\\\"`. The fix to `find_item()` acknowledges this principle by deduplicating matches when all share the same issue number \u2014 recognizing that when a title substring matches multiple items, the issue number is the tiebreaker. The secondary fix to align line 648 with line 234's pattern (using issue number instead of title) strengthens consistency with the documented design principle that identifiers must be stable and platform-independent. No contradiction found.\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 line 393 uses `selector='{title}'` in a Mermaid diagram for `backlog_update`; line 645 already recommends `selector=\\\"#{N}\\\"` for the verified step. The diagram on line 393 perpetuates the fragile title-selector pattern and should note that issue-number selectors are preferred when available. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: AI agents reading this diagram may follow the title-selector pattern shown, reproducing the bug on items with diverged local cache titles.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` \u2014 multiple `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", ...)` examples (lines 139, 142, 146, 147, 159, 167, 168, 206). These examples normalize the title-selector pattern as the expected usage. Should note that `selector=\\\"#{N}\\\"` is the robust form when an issue number is available. | Risk: LOW | Why: Examples, not instructions; does not actively trigger the bug but reinforces the fragile pattern.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n\\n### Grooming Notes\\n\\nValidation gate warning (2026-05-25): Sections written as `RT-ICA` and `Fact-Check` are stored in YAML as `rt_ica` and `fact_check` (snake_case normalization). The backlog server does not reverse-map these keys when building sections_index or the sections dict, so they do not appear in `backlog_view` output. Content is fully present in local YAML and synced to GitHub. Root cause is the same class of identifier-normalization inconsistency described in the bug body \u2014 section names with hyphens but no spaces are converted to snake_case. See #2459 scope: parsing.py normalization logic may need to handle section keys as well as selectors. Sections affected by this groom: `RT-ICA` (stored as `rt_ica`, Decision: APPROVED) and `Fact-Check` (stored as `fact_check`, 4 VERIFIED + 1 INCONCLUSIVE).\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n\\n**Rationale**: The selector resolution logic in `backlog_core/parsing.py:find_item()` raises AmbiguousSelectorError whenever a substring match produces multiple items, without checking if those items resolve to the same GitHub issue number. Title divergence between local cache and GitHub produces duplicate BacklogItem entries for the same issue, causing false ambiguity errors on valid selectors.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys root-cause analysis via source code inspection\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Substring selector matching multiple cached items with same issue number raises AmbiguousSelectorError \u2192 backlog_update accepts selector when all matches deduplicate to single issue\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::find_item` \u2014 the fix site; incorrect dedup logic silently returns wrong item or fails to fix the bug\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` line 648 \u2014 direct trigger; observed in production; agent will hit this again on any item with prefix divergence\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` \u2014 no regression guard exists for the same-issue-number dedup case\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - behavioral (selector matching semantics change \u2014 must preserve all-different-issues-raise invariant)\\n  - agent-instruction (6 workflow files instruct title-based selectors that are fragile against local-cache/GitHub title divergence)\\n  - verification (missing test for the specific same-issue-number dedup scenario)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - 5 additional work-backlog-item workflow files use title-based selectors and are exposed to the same failure mode, though the bug was first observed in complete-implementation. These are secondary blast radius beyond the original item description.\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys via /find-cause skill with source code verification\\n\\n**Evidence chain**:\\n\\n1. Observed failure: `backlog_update(selector=\\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content\\\", verified=True)` raised AmbiguousSelectorError listing two matches, both with issue number #2437\\n\\n2. Why: The substring selector \\\"add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content\\\" matched two BacklogItem entries in the cached items list via case-insensitive substring comparison (line 24 of parsing.py)\\n\\n3. Why: Both BacklogItem entries have the same title prefix but different full titles: one includes \\\"refactor:\\\" prefix from GitHub issue, the other from local cache \u2014 both represent the same GitHub issue #2437\\n\\n4. Why: The local backlog cache is not auto-synced when GitHub issue titles change after cache creation, so cached copies diverge from canonical titles stored in GitHub\\n\\n5. Why: The `find_item()` selector resolution algorithm checks `if len(matches) > 1: raise AmbiguousSelectorError()` without first deduplicating matches by their GitHub issue number property \u2014 it treats any match count > 1 as ambiguous, even when all matches point to the same issue\\n\\n**Root cause**: The selector matching algorithm in `find_item()` raises ambiguity errors without deduplicating cached entries that resolve to the same GitHub issue number. When cache titles diverge from GitHub canonical titles, duplicate BacklogItem entries for the same issue trigger false ambiguity.\\n\\n**Fix locus**: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py:find_item()` lines 24-26 \u2014 after substring matching, group matches by issue number and deduplicate before checking ambiguity\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py` | Role: producer | Connection: `find_item` is the selector resolution function that produces the ambiguous error; fix resides here | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'AmbiguousSelectorError'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` | Role: other-reference | Connection: defines `AmbiguousSelectorError` class raised by find_item | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: 7 functions call `find_item`; all benefit from fix; update_item is the direct path for backlog_update | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM | pattern: 'find_item'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: MCP interface for `backlog_update`; catches BacklogError generically; transparent fix | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` | Role: test | Connection: guards `find_item` behavior; missing test for same-issue-number dedup scenario | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` | Role: test | Connection: integration tests for `backlog_update`; no test for dual-representation ambiguity | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: line 648 instructs `selector=\\\"{matched_item_title}\\\"` \u2014 direct trigger of the observed bug | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'matched_item_title'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: lines 115, 127 use title-based selectors for plan linking | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/github-sync.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", status=\\\"in-progress\\\")` | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", plan=\\\"quick-{slug}\\\")` | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/plan.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Mermaid diagram uses `selector={title}` for backlog_update | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: instructs `backlog_update(selector=\\\"{title}\\\", status=\\\"in-progress\\\")` | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: line 393 Mermaid diagram shows title-based selector; line 645 correctly recommends #N form | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: multiple examples using `selector=\\\"{title}\\\"` normalize the fragile pattern | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_find_item.py` \u2014 covers `AmbiguousSelectorError` when multiple **distinct-issue** items match (lines 62\u201391). Does NOT cover the new scenario: two items matching the same title substring with the **same issue number** should not raise \u2014 this is the root regression. New test case required for `TestFindItemByTitleSubstring`: assert that when all matches share the same issue number, `find_item` returns (not raises). | Risk: HIGH | Why: Without this test, the fix has no regression guard. Existing tests remain valid (different-issue ambiguity still raises).\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` \u2014 tests `backlog_update` end-to-end (lines 188\u2013203). Tests title-based selectors against a single item. No test exercises a local cache containing two representations of the same issue that both match a given substring. A new scenario test is needed. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Integration coverage gap for the exact failure mode described in the bug report.\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **5 claims evaluated**\\n- **4 VERIFIED** \u2014 All core selector behavior and workflow claims are supported by source code\\n- **1 INCONCLUSIVE** \u2014 Cache/GitHub title divergence is architecturally possible but not explicitly confirmed in the codebase\\n\\nThe root cause of issue #2459 (ambiguous selector with same issue number) is verified as technically possible: when multiple local items exist with overlapping title substrings, both pointing to the same GitHub issue, `backlog_update` with a substring selector will raise `AmbiguousSelectorError`. The complete-implementation skill's documented workflow (backlog_list \u2192 backlog_update with matched title) is confirmed to create this risk when title formats diverge between cache sources.\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:e6b4f9ce1899aaf7cf28eb5536b7b3d0 -->\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"31c2e2bc7ce9ad71e5992ba79881d32afdad9ef8d587e9318f0547d73c57d3ed\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCPhug\"}"}